# LIVE REGRESSION FIXTURE — the trait-object pack must fire at EVERY builtin
# `Vector` consuming position, not just at the call-argument positions that
# carry a real signature.
#
# THE CLASS: `method_param_types` in `lower_method_call` is filled ONLY on the
# `is_gir_method` path and is EMPTY for every builtin collection method, so
# `callee_param_type` at `lower_call_arg` is `None` and the pack takes its
# `None => return val` early exit — it was never asked the question at all.
# (`fn_sigs` is NOT consulted at this site. It would answer `I64_TYPE`, which
# is why a destination-type carrier had to be ADDED rather than repaired, but
# the pack never sees that answer. Regenerate:
# `grep -n 'is_gir_method\|method_param_types' src/ir/lowering/exprs/methods.rs`.)
# A `Box[Concrete]` then reached
# `Vector__Box__Speaker__set` unpacked, and the runtime memcpy'd 16 bytes of
# `{data, vtable}` out of an 8-byte `void*` slot. Every row below was rc 135
# before the destination type was routed through the protocol table.
#
# ⚠ ONE ROW PER `Vector` SIBLING — AND THAT IS ALL THIS FILE CLAIMS
# (Core #4 / Core #12). `push` alone is one value of a typed axis; `set`,
# `insert` and `fill` are the same position class reached through three more
# `BuiltinMethodDecl` arms. `fill` and `get_or_put` are deliberately NOT
# consuming positions — they take the destination type as a HINT only — so they
# also pin that the hint path and the consume path read the same carrier.
#
# ⚠ SIBLING PROTOCOLS ARE PINNED ELSEWHERE, NOT IMPLIED HERE. The value-writing
# position class spans more receivers than `Vector`, and each has its own file:
# `box_trait_pack_map_consuming_positions.gg` (`Dict`/`HashMap`
# `put`/`get_or_put`), `box_trait_pack_set_consuming_positions.gg`
# (`Set`/`HashSet` `add`/`insert` — the ONE-ARGUMENT shape, which an earlier
# revision of this class fix silently omitted while this header claimed sibling
# totality), `box_trait_pack_deque_alias_push.gg` (the alias axis) and
# `box_trait_pack_index_assign.gg` (`v[i] = x` / `d[k] = x`, a different write
# site). Regenerate the family from the declaration table, never from this
# list:  grep -n 'name: "put"\|name: "set"\|name: "insert"\|name: "fill"\|name: "get_or_put"' \
#            src/ir/lowering/builtins.rs
#
# ⚠ THE PUSHED VALUE MUST BE A TEMP. A `Box` is single-owner-by-design, so a
# live named source at these positions is `E_MoveWithoutOperator` at check
# time; the temp form is the only shape that reaches the pack here.
#
# ⚠ Payloads are heap-forced through `mk`. A literal would not reallocate and
# would hide a stale-pointer read.
#
# ⚠ It stays in `known_gaps/` deliberately: moving the file registers a
# non-MATCH against `RUNTIME_DIFF_NONMATCH_CEILING`, and Core #9 ⊕ forbids
# raising that ceiling for a round's own inflow. The self-host lane has no
# analogue of the GIR-level pack at consuming positions — its
# `try_emit_trait_obj_construct` mirrors the Rust LIR Assign/SlotStore
# coercion only — and still crashes on this shape.
#
# INTENDED: prints `C3!`, `B2!`, `F6!` on both backends.
from std.collections import Box
from std.collections import Vector

trait Speaker:
    String speak(self)

struct Robot:
    String id

equip Robot with Speaker:
    String speak(self):
        return self.id + "!"

String mk(String a, String b):
    return a + b

void main():
    Vector[Box[Speaker]] v = Vector[Box[Speaker]]()
    v.push(Box.new(Robot(mk("A", "1"))))
    v.set(0, Box.new(Robot(mk("B", "2"))))
    v.insert(0, Box.new(Robot(mk("C", "3"))))
    print(v[0].speak())
    print(v[1].speak())

    Vector[Box[Speaker]] f = Vector[Box[Speaker]]()
    f.fill(2, Box.new(Robot(mk("F", "6"))))
    print(f[1].speak())
